General elections were held in [[Ecuador]] on 19 May 1996, with a second round of the presidential elections on 7 July.<ref name=N1>[[Dieter Nohlen]] (2005) ''Elections in the Americas: A data handbook, Volume II'', p379 {{ISBN|978-0-19-928358-3}}</ref><ref>{{Cite journal |last=Barczak |first=Monica |date=1997-03-01 |title=The 1996 Ecuadorian elections |url=https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0261379497831216 |journal=Electoral Studies |volume=16 |issue=1 |pages=107–117 |doi=10.1016/S0261-3794(97)83121-6 |issn=0261-3794|url-access=subscription }}</ref> Although [[Jaime Nebot]] of the [[Social Christian Party (Ecuador)|Social Christian Party]] received the most votes in the first round, [[Abdalá Bucaram]] of the [[Ecuadorian Roldosist Party]] won the run-off with 54.5% of the vote.<ref>Nohlen, p404</ref> The Social Christian Party remained the largest in the Chamber of Deputies, winning 27 of the 82 seats.<ref>Nohlen, p1996</ref>

Until the [[2013 Ecuadorian general election|2013 vote]], this was the last election held after the natural expiration of a four-year presidential term. This due to a decade of political [[1998–99 Ecuador banking crisis|and economical]] instability that Ecuador experienced after Bucaram was impeached by the [[Congress of Ecuador|Congress]] in early 1997 and that lasted until [[Rafael Correa]]'s inauguration in early 2007.<ref name="LaRepública">[http://www.larepublica.ec/blog/politica/2014/01/17/los-7-anos-de-correa-en-ecuador-estabilidad-en-medio-de-exitos-y-criticas/ "Los 7 años de Correa en Ecuador: estabilidad en medio de éxitos y críticas"] January 17, 2014. ''La República''</ref>

